<div class="exercise"><div class="ex-q"><p>Why does the “add 12 uniforms” trick never produce values beyond ±6?</p>
</div><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><div class="ex-s"><p>Each uniform is in [0, 1], so the sum lies in [0, 12] and the standardised value in [−6, 6]. A true Gaussian exceeds 6σ with probability about <span class="katex"><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">2</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.8141em;"></span><span class="mord">1</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">0</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height:0.8141em;"><span style="top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height:2.7em;"></span><span class="katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mtight">−</span><span class="mord mtight">9</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, so the truncation is irrelevant for most simulations but matters if you’re estimating very rare events.</p>
</div></details></div>
